ProsperoSunshine: the keyboard and mouse of a client without a controller as a pad of its own

A friend who joins from a laptop without a controller sees the game but can't play: their keys and mouse go to the one
virtual keyboard and mouse that every client shares, which work in the console's menus and in few games. While a
stream's client has no controller, its key and mouse events now go to src/input/keyboard_pad.hpp instead, which turns
them into a gamepad state with one of its profiles, and from there to a pad of its own in gamepads[0]. The pad is
added on the first press of a key or mouse button that is one of its controls, the way a controller is (alloc_id,
platf::alloc_gamepad), so it takes the lowest free slot and counts toward the four, and goes neutral and away with the
stream's other pads. The mouse's movement and the wheel alone don't add it, so a client that only watches takes no
player slot. Only input.cpp knows which stream an event comes from: the platform layer's keyboard and mouse calls
don't get the client.

The client's first controller takes that pad over, slot, user and all, so the console doesn't ask who uses it again;
from then on the keyboard and mouse go to the shared ones. When that controller goes, its pad goes with it and the
console says so; the next key takes a pad again. A key or button this client pressed on the shared ones before still
comes up there. With no pad free they go to the shared ones too, the press that found none and its release with them,
until a control press 10 s later gets a pad, or the client's next stream. Like any pad, it plays as the host or as a
guest by its client (patch 0016, src/input/pad_roles.hpp), and a guest's Create and PS don't go through.

sunshine.conf: keyboard_mouse_pad = 0 turns it off, keyboard_mouse_profile picks a profile (standard unless set),
keyboard_mouse_sensitivity (percent) is where a client's mouse starts. Ctrl+Alt with the wheel changes the sensitivity
a step at a time while playing; the host keeps it per client (a hash of its identity) and profile in
keyboard_sensitivity.txt and shows nothing on the console, which everyone streaming would see.

